# ANISOTROPY TEST (reduced sigma_t' ~= 2.1 -- anisotropic but SHALLOW, the other side of the test).
#
# Generated from _fog_thick.ftsl with ONLY the medium line changed -- see VOLCACHE.
# Every thick-medium result in that entry is at g 0.0, so the claim "the order >= 2
# field is smooth on thick media" carries an unstated ISOTROPIC condition. The
# similarity relation sigma_s' = sigma_s (1 - g) predicts the smoothness follows the
# REDUCED optical depth, which would mean the scattering-order histogram is the right
# predictor precisely because it already folds g in.
# Optically THICK Cornell box — the scene that can tell mode J (UPBP) apart from mode D.
#
# `_fog_cornell.ftsl` is the CORRECTNESS gate: sigma_t 0.6 across a 1 m box is an optical
# depth of 0.6, which mode D's connections sample perfectly well, so mode J provably agrees
# with mode D there and provably buys nothing (measured 0.218.0: identical noise for ~25x
# the time). That is the right outcome for a correctness scene and a useless one for a
# PERFORMANCE claim — you cannot show an estimator rescuing a technique that was never in
# trouble.
#
# This is the same box with the one variable that matters turned up: sigma_t 20, an optical
# depth of ~20 across the box, with a high albedo so the light actually survives to scatter
# many times instead of simply being absorbed. That is the regime UPBP exists for. A BDPT
# connection needs the CAMERA's free-flight distance sample to land on the scattering point
# and then needs the shadow ray to survive exp(-tau) back to the light; at tau ~ 20 both are
# hopeless, so mode D's deep glow is pure noise. A photon beam is a whole LINE of deposited
# power, so a camera ray that passes anywhere within the kernel radius collects it — the
# merge reaches where the connection cannot.
#
# The medium is BOUNDED to the box on purpose. `_fog_cornell`'s haze is unbounded, which is
# harmless at sigma_t 0.6 but at sigma_t 20 would put an optical depth of ~44 between the
# camera (z = 2.7) and the box and render a black frame.
#
#   ftrace -in scenes/_fog_thick.ftsl -mode D -device cpu -r 64 -time 300 -hdr -o png/thick_d.png
#   ftrace -in scenes/_fog_thick.ftsl -mode J -device cpu -r 64 -time 300 -hdr -o png/thick_j.png

scene {
    units    meters
    spectral 360 830 1
}

material "white" { type diffuse    reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "red"   { type diffuse    reflect redwall }
material "green" { type diffuse    reflect greenwall }
material "glass" { type dielectric ior    glass:SF10 }

quad { origin 0 0 0   u 1 0 0   v 0 0 1   material white }   # floor
quad { origin 0 1 0   u 0 0 1   v 1 0 0   material white }   # ceiling
quad { origin 0 0 0   u 0 1 0   v 1 0 0   material white }   # back
quad { origin 0 0 0   u 0 0 1   v 0 1 0   material red   }   # left
quad { origin 1 0 0   u 0 1 0   v 0 0 1   material green }   # right

sphere { center 0.5 0.32 0.4   radius 0.25   material glass }

# The one difference from _fog_cornell, plus the bounds that make it renderable at all.
medium {
    sigma_t 20.0   albedo 0.995   g 0.9
    bounds { min 0 0 0   max 1 1 1 }
}

light area {
    origin 0.35 0.999 0.35   u 0.3 0 0   v 0 0 0.3   normal 0 -1 0
    spd preset:bb6500
}

camera "cam" {
    eye 0.5 0.5 2.7   look_at 0.5 0.5 0.5   up 0 1 0   fov_y 40
    mode B
    film { res 256 256 }
}
